From 579cd0bc89550856dfaa9e7c00c972d5c957162b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mjb-is <mjb-is@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:27:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 091/120] Back up and restore alternate data streams and NTFS
 extended attributes

pxar carries a file's main data only, so folder backups on Windows lose
every alternate data stream (Zone.Identifier, which drives the "this file
came from the Internet" warning, and streams other programs keep) and every
NTFS extended attribute (WSL keeps Linux owners and modes in them). TODO.md
lists streams under "NTFS Metadata Fidelity"; the Restore page has had a
disabled "Restore Alternate Data Streams (ADS)" option and
RestoreOptions.RestoreADS has been a logged no-op.

Backup (Windows)
- The NTFS metadata collector opens each file and folder once, without
  data access (READ_CONTROL | FILE_READ_EA | FILE_READ_ATTRIBUTES), and
  reads the security descriptor, attributes, named $DATA streams
  (FileStreamInfo) and extended attributes (NtQueryEaFile) on that one
  handle. Reading the streams and EAs on a second handle took the cost per
  entry from about 90 to 185 us; one handle costs about 128 us. Reparse
  points keep the lookups by name, as before, and record no streams.
- New optional side file fields FileMetaEntry.Streams ("t") and EAs ("e");
  older clients ignore them. Stream content is recorded up to 1 MiB per
  stream and 8 MiB per archive; a larger stream, one over the budget or one
  that cannot be read is recorded by name and size with the reason, and is
  not recreated on restore. $KERNEL.* EAs, which only Windows may set, are
  left out.
- Finalize serializes with serializeFileMetaCapped: over 15 MiB (PBS
  refuses blobs over 16 MiB) the stream contents are dropped and logged, so
  permissions and attributes are never lost to a large stream.
- Buffers for FileStreamInfo and the EA calls are 8-byte aligned: with a
  misaligned buffer the call fails with ERROR_NOACCESS, and a []byte from
  make is aligned only some of the time, so it failed at random.
- The backup log counts the streams recorded, those recorded by name only,
  and the EAs.

Restore
- With RestoreADS set (the Restore page option, now enabled and ticked by
  default), the metadata pass writes each file's and folder's streams and
  EAs back first, while the file is still writable, then sets its
  modification time back to what the extraction gave it (writing a stream
  changes it), then the creation time, then the attributes and ACL.
  Writing a stream on a file does not change its folder's time.
- RestoreADS off writes no streams and no EAs. Elsewhere than Windows it is
  a no-op.

Tests: unit tests on NTFS for the round trip through the gzipped side
file (file and folder streams, an empty stream, a stream over 1 MiB
recorded by name only, EAs, the same SDDL and attributes as the lookups by
name, the parent folder's time untouched), the budget, the blob cap, the
aligned buffer and the EA buffer layout; an opt-in live test (PBS_E2E=1)
that backs up a folder with streams, EAs, a read-only file and old times
and restores it with streams and ACLs, streams without ACLs, and streams
off.

